"\"\<\!\(\*StyleBox[\"Connection\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"to\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*\
StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"Part\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\
\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"a\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*\
StyleBox[\".\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\) \n  For small \[Beta], sinh 2\[Beta] \
\[TildeTilde] 2\[Beta], so g\:2080\:2084 \[TildeTilde] \[Minus]2c \
\!\(\*SubscriptBox[\(p\), \(4\)]\) m\[Beta]/r. That is Part\n  a\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s \[CurlyEpsilon] c p\:2084 h(r) with h = \
\!\(\*SubscriptBox[\(C\), \(2\)]\)/r and \
\!\(\*SubscriptBox[\(\[CurlyEpsilon]C\), \(2\)]\) = \[Minus]2m\[Beta]. The \
linear Coulomb\n  solution of Part a is the small-\[Beta] limit of this exact \
solution.\n\n\!\(\*StyleBox[\"Not\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"the\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*\
StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"ordinary\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\
\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"Reissner\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\
\!\(\*StyleBox[\"\[Dash]\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"Nordstr\
\[ODoubleDot]m\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"black\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\
\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"hole\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\).\n  \
\!\(\*SubscriptBox[\(D\), \(4\)]\) now depends on r, so the circle\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s size varies with r. In 4D \n  language, that varying size \
is a scalar field (the dilaton).\n  \
