    "Print", "[", "\"\<\!\(\*StyleBox[\"Notes\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\):\>\"",
      "]"}], ";"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
    "Print", "[", "\"\<\!\(\*StyleBox[\"\[Beta]\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\
\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"=\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*\
StyleBox[\" \",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"0\",FontWeight->\"Bold\
\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\":\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"the\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*\
StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"uncharged\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\
\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"black\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\
\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"string\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\
\(\*StyleBox[\":\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\) \n  With \[Beta] = 0, A = \
\!\(\*SuperscriptBox[\(c\), \(2\)]\)(1 \[Minus] 2m/r), \!\(\*SubscriptBox[\(D\
\), \(4\)]\) = 1 and \!\(\*SubscriptBox[\(g\), \(04\)]\) = 0. That is the\n  \
Schwarzschild metric times a fixed circle, the same background\n  used in \
Part a. It is called a black string because the horizon\n  at r = 2m extends \
around the whole circle.\n\n\
\!\(\*StyleBox[\"Why\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"it\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*\
StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"is\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*\
StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"Ricci\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\
\*StyleBox[\"-\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"flat\",FontWeight->\"\
Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"for\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*\
StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"every\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\
\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"\[Beta]\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\
\(\*StyleBox[\":\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\) \n  Write \n\tT = ct and Y = \
\!\(\*SubscriptBox[\(p\), \(4\)]\)\[Psi].\n  The uncharged string is then\n\t\
\!\(\*SuperscriptBox[\(ds\), \(2\)]\) = \!\(\*SuperscriptBox[\(dT\), \(2\)]\) \
\[Minus] \!\(\*SuperscriptBox[\(dY\), \(2\)]\) \[Minus] (2m/r) \
\!\(\*SuperscriptBox[\(dT\), \(2\)]\) \[Minus] (spatial part).\n\n  Now apply \
a Lorentz \!\(\*SuperscriptBox[\(boost\), \((1)\)]\):\n\tT = T\[Prime] cosh \
\[Beta] + Y\[Prime] sinh \[Beta]\n\tY = T\[Prime] sinh \[Beta] + Y\[Prime] \
cosh \[Beta]\n\n  The term \!\(\*SuperscriptBox[\(dT\), \(2\)]\) \[Minus] \
\!\(\*SuperscriptBox[\(dY\), \(2\)]\) doesn\[CloseCurlyQuote]t change under \
the boost. The term \[Minus](2m/r)\!\(\*SuperscriptBox[\(dT\), \(2\)]\)\n  \
becomes \[Minus](2m/r)(cosh \[Beta] dT\[Prime] + sinh \[Beta] \
dY\[Prime]\!\(\*SuperscriptBox[\()\), \(2\)]\). Expanding it gives exactly\n  \
the Part b metric:\n\t\!\(\*SubscriptBox[\(g\), \(00\)]\) = \
\!\(\*SuperscriptBox[\(c\), \(2\)]\)(1 \[Minus] \!\(\*FractionBox[\(2  m\\\ \
\*SuperscriptBox[\(cosh\), \(2\)] \[Beta]\), \(r\)]\))\n\t\!\(\*SubscriptBox[\
\(g\), \(44\)]\) = \[Minus]\!\(\*SuperscriptBox[SubscriptBox[\(p\), \(4\)], \
\(2\)]\)(1 + \!\(\*FractionBox[\(2  m\\\ \*SuperscriptBox[\(sinh\), \(2\)] \
\[Beta]\), \(r\)]\))\n\t\!\(\*SubscriptBox[\(g\), \(04\)]\) = \
\[Minus]\!\(\*FractionBox[\(c\\\ \*SubscriptBox[\(p\), \(4\)]\\\ m\\\ sinh\\\ \
2  \[Beta]\), \(r\)]\)\n\n  A coordinate change can\[CloseCurlyQuote]t create \
curvature. So \!\(\*SubscriptBox[\(R\), \(\[Mu]\[Nu]\)]\) = 0 for every\n  \
\[Beta], which is what the notebook confirms.\n\n\
\!\(\*StyleBox[\"\[Beta]\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"is\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*\
StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"the\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*\
StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*SuperscriptBox[StyleBox[\"rapidity\",\
FontWeight->\"Bold\"], RowBox[{\"(\", \"1\", \")\"}]]\): \n  The boost \
velocity along the circle is v = c tanh \[Beta]. Cosh and sinh \n  appear \
because a boost keeps T\.b2\[Minus]Y\.b2 fixed, just as a rotation keeps \n  \
x\.b2+y\.b2 fixed using cos and sin. \n\n\!\(\*StyleBox[\"This\",FontWeight->\
\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*SuperscriptBox[StyleBox[\"boost\",FontWeight->\
\"Bold\"], RowBox[{\"(\", \"1\", \")\"}]]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"is\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*\
StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"not\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*\
StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"simply\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\
\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"a\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*\
StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"coordinate\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\
\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"change\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\
\(\*StyleBox[\":\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\n  Because \[Psi] is periodic, \
\[Psi] and \[Psi]+2\[Pi] are the same point, but the boost \n  gives them two \
different times t\[Prime]. So the boost is a coordinate \n  change only \
locally; globally, the boosted string is a different \n  spacetime.\n\n  The \
boosted string has angular\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \",
FontVariations->{\"Underline\"->True}]\)momentum\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \",
FontVariations->{\"Underline\"->True}]\)because, relative to \n  the original \
frame, it moves around the circle.\n\n\n(Footnote)\n1\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"Boost\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\
\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"and\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*\
StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"Rapidity\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\) \
\[Dash] A rotation in 3-space is a coordinate change\n  where the rotated \
coordinates are represented in terms of an\n  angular change, say \[Theta].\n\
\n  Space and time coordinates cannot be rotated into each other the\n  way \
space axes can. Mixing them gives a boost (cosh, sinh) instead\n  of a \
rotation (cos, sin).\n\n  Like a rotation, a boost can be a change of \
coordinates, but it is \n  described by velocity instead of an angle. The \
size of a boost,\n  say \[Beta], is called a rapidity. It is a \\\"hyperbolic \
angle\\\". The \n  corresponding speed is v=c tanh \[Beta].\>\"", "]"}], 
    ";"}], "\n", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
    "Print", "[", "\"\<\n\n\n\!\(\*StyleBox[\"Boost\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\
\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"represents\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\
\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"electric\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\
\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"charge\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\
\(\*StyleBox[\":\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\) \n  In Kaluza-Klein theory, \
momentum around the circle is electric \n  charge. So boosting the uncharged \
string makes it charged, with\n  charge proportional to m sinh 2\[Beta].\>\"",
      "]"}], ";"}]}]}]], "Input",
